<h2>The litigation before the settlement</h2>
<p>The parties married in December 2018; a daughter was born a year later. As the marriage collapsed, the wife lodged an FIR at Jamalpur police station in 2022 invoking, among others, Sections 498A, 307, 406 and 323 IPC, and then petitioned for divorce on grounds of cruelty and adultery. The Family Court at Munger dismissed her divorce petition in October 2025 — leaving the parties formally married, criminally embroiled, and factually estranged. Her appeal to the High Court under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act followed.</p>

<h2>From contest to compromise</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>July 2026:</strong> The wife files I.A. No. 1 of 2026 expressing readiness for a one-time settlement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>28 October 2026:</strong> The parties file a joint compromise petition recording agreed terms.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>In court:</strong> The husband hands over a demand draft of Rs 5,00,000 (DD No. 121963 dated 31 October 2026).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2 November 2026:</strong> The court disposes of the appeal on the compromise — without entering the merits.</div>
</div>

<h2>The orders made</h2>
<p>Three consequences flowed in a single judgment. First, the criminal side: further proceedings in Jamalpur P.S. Case No. 140 of 2022, pending before the JMFC, Munger, were quashed and closed (para 9). Second, mutual peace: both parties are bound to &ldquo;abide by the terms and conditions of settlement and in future they will not file any case against each other relating to their matrimonial dispute&rdquo; (para 11). Third, status: &ldquo;The marriage between the parties is dissolved&rdquo;; the Family Court&rsquo;s judgment was set aside and the appeal allowed on compromise, with a direction that the decree be drawn (paras 12-13).</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Why route the settlement through the appeal?</strong> Because it produces a decree of dissolution — not merely a withdrawal. Had the appeal simply been withdrawn, the Family Court&rsquo;s dismissal would stand and the parties would remain married, their settlement a private arrangement. By setting aside the judgment below and dissolving the marriage on the compromise, the High Court gave the settlement the one thing only a court can give: a change of status, final and enforceable.</p>
</div>

<h2>Settlement design in matrimonial appeals</h2>
<p>Comprehensive matrimonial settlements typically bundle four elements, all visible here: a one-time monetary payment in a verifiable instrument handed over before the court; dissolution of the marriage; quashing of pending criminal proceedings that are, in substance, facets of the matrimonial dispute; and mutual non-litigation undertakings recorded in the order. Each element protects a different party against a different risk — the wife against default on the payment, the husband against revival of prosecutions, and both against the next decade of litigation.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Record payment in court: a demand draft tendered and noted in the order leaves no execution dispute later.</li>
  <li>List every pending case — criminal, maintenance, DV — in the compromise petition so the quashing and closure sweep is complete; a case left unmentioned is a case left alive.</li>
  <li>Non-litigation clauses relate to the matrimonial dispute; they are not a licence for future wrongs, and they bind best when reproduced in the court&rsquo;s own order, as here.</li>
  <li>Where a divorce petition was dismissed below, structure the settlement as an appellate disposal setting aside the decree — not a withdrawal — so the dissolution takes effect as a decree of court.</li>
</ul>
</div>
